@* macOS: the main program.
\label{macmain}The Windows main program creates a window with the Win32 interface and runs
a loop that alternates between processing the messages of the window and drawing a frame.
A Mac program is organized the other way round. The application object of AppKit,
|NSApplication|, owns the loop; the program provides a \emph{delegate}, an object whose
methods the application calls when something happens: the program has started, a window
is closing, the program is about to quit. The drawing is driven the same way, by a
|MTKView|---a view of MetalKit that owns the drawable images of the window (the
counterpart of the swap chain) and asks its own delegate to draw once per refresh of the
display.

AppKit is an Objective-C interface, so this one file is written in Objective-C++, the
mixture of C++ and Objective-C that Apple's compiler accepts in files ending in
\texttt{.mm}. Two pieces of Objective-C syntax are enough to read it:
|[object message:argument]| sends a message, that is, calls a method; and
|@interface|/|@implementation| declare and define a class. Objective-C references to Metal
objects (|id<MTLDevice>|) and metal-cpp pointers (|MTL::Device*|) designate the same
objects, so converting one into the other is a simple cast, written |(__bridge T)p|.

<<file:mac/src/main.mm>>=
// Texture Explorer (macOS) - an interactive Metal tool for learning about texturing:
// color, bump, displacement and roughness maps, UV space and tangent-space normals.
// This is the only Objective-C++ file: it creates the window and runs the frame loop.
// Everything else is C++, using Metal through metal-cpp.
#include "App.h"
#include <imgui.h>
#include <imgui_impl_metal.h>
#include <imgui_impl_osx.h>
#import <Cocoa/Cocoa.h>
#import <MetalKit/MetalKit.h>
#include <chrono>
#include <string>

